{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Magnetometer and gradiometer channels exhibit different word-evoked MEG amplitudes: one sensor type yields systematically larger mean responses.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare evoked MEG amplitude for word onsets between magnetometers and gradiometers.",  
      "steps":"1. Load all sessions’ channel_types attribute and parse into an array parallel to channel_names (values “mag” or “grad”).\n2. For each session, read events.tsv and select kind=='word'.\n3. For each word event, epoch MEG data from –0.1 to +0.3 s around timemeg, baseline-correct using –0.1–0 s.\n4. Compute mean evoked amplitude in the 0.05–0.2 s window separately for mag channels and for grad channels.\n5. Pool per-trial mag and grad amplitudes across sessions into two vectors.\n6. Perform a paired statistical test (e.g., paired t-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank) comparing mag vs grad amplitudes.\n7. Visualize boxplots of amplitude by sensor type and report statistical summary.","deliverables":"1. Test statistic, p-value, and effect size for mag vs grad amplitude difference.\n2. Boxplot or violin plot of evoked amplitude for magnetometers and gradiometers.\n3. Table of mean ± SD amplitudes by sensor type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Words with more phonemes evoke larger MEG responses due to increased phonological processing demands.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between phonemic length of a word and its evoked MEG amplitude.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session’s events.tsv, group phoneme rows by wordidx and sentenceidx to count phonemes per word.\n2. Merge counts with the corresponding word-level events (kind=='word') to obtain phoneme_count and timemeg per word.\n3. For each word, epoch MEG data (–0.1 to +0.3 s), baseline-correct, and compute mean amplitude in the 0.05–0.2 s window across all channels.\n4. Compile a table of (phoneme_count, evoked_amplitude) across sessions.\n5. Fit a linear regression of evoked_amplitude ~ phoneme_count; report slope, confidence interval, and p-value.\n6. Plot scatter of amplitude vs phoneme count with regression line and histogram of phoneme counts.","deliverables":"1. Regression summary (slope, CI, p-value) for phoneme count effect.\n2. Scatter plot with fit line.\n3. Histogram of phoneme counts per word."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shorter inter-word intervals (time between end of previous word and next word onset) attenuate the following word’s evoked MEG amplitude due to neural refractory dynamics.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the duration between consecutive words modulates MEG word-evoked responses.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session’s events.tsv, extract all word events sorted by timemeg.\n2. For each word (except the first), compute inter_word_interval = timemeg_current – (timemeg_previous + duration_previous).\n3. Exclude trials with negative or extreme intervals (e.g., <0 or >2 s).\n4. Epoch MEG around each word (–0.1 to +0.3 s), baseline-correct, and compute mean amplitude in 0.05–0.2 s.\n5. Compile pairs (inter_word_interval, evoked_amplitude) across sessions.\n6. Fit a nonlinear model (e.g., exponential recovery) or linear regression of amplitude on interval; assess goodness-of-fit.\n7. Plot amplitude vs interval with fitted curve and histogram of intervals.","deliverables":"1. Model parameters, confidence intervals, and fit statistics (R²).\n2. Scatter plot with fitted recovery curve.\n3. Histogram of inter-word intervals."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"With increasing session number (1–10), mean word-evoked MEG amplitude decreases due to participant habituation or fatigue effects.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine trends in average word-evoked amplitude across recording sessions.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session index 1 through 10, load that session’s events.tsv and MEG data.\n2. Extract all word epochs (–0.1 to +0.3 s), baseline-correct, and compute mean amplitude in 0.05–0.2 s, averaged across channels and trials to obtain session_mean_amplitude.\n3. Create a vector of session_mean_amplitudes vs session_number.\n4. Perform a trend analysis (e.g., linear regression or Spearman correlation) of amplitude vs session number.\n5. Plot session_mean_amplitude against session number with fitted line.","deliverables":"1. Trend test result (slope or correlation coefficient, p-value).\n2. Line plot of mean amplitude vs session number with confidence band.\n3. Table of session means ± SD."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Pre-onset beta-band (13–30 Hz) power predicts subsequent word-evoked MEG amplitude: higher beta power leads to attenuated responses.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate the relationship between pre-stimulus beta power and evoked amplitude at word onset.",  
      "steps":"1. Load all word events and MEG data across sessions.\n2. For each word event, extract MEG data from –0.2 to 0 s around timemeg, bandpass-filter in 13–30 Hz, apply Hilbert transform, and average analytic amplitude over time and channels to get pre_beta_power.\n3. Epoch MEG from 0.05 to 0.2 s, baseline-correct using –0.1 to 0 s, and compute mean evoked amplitude across channels.\n4. Aggregate (pre_beta_power, evoked_amplitude) pairs.\n5. Fit linear regression: evoked_amplitude ~ pre_beta_power; report slope, CI, and p-value.\n6. Plot scatter with regression line and histogram of pre-onset beta power.","deliverables":"1. Regression summary for beta power effect.\n2. Scatter plot with fit line.\n3. Histogram of pre-onset beta power."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Vowel phonemes evoke larger MEG responses than consonant phonemes due to greater cortical engagement in vowel processing.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare evoked MEG amplitudes at vowel versus consonant phoneme onsets.",  
      "steps":"1. Define sets of ARPABET vowel labels (e.g., AA, AE, AH, AO, EH, ER, EY, IH, IY, OW, UH, UW) and treat all other phoneme labels as consonants.\n2. For each session’s events.tsv, select kind=='phoneme' and classify each segment as vowel or consonant based on segment label.\n3. For each phoneme onset, epoch MEG (–0.1 to +0.3 s), baseline-correct, and compute mean amplitude in the 0.05–0.2 s window averaged across channels.\n4. Pool amplitudes separately for vowel and consonant trials.\n5. Perform a between-group statistical test (e.g., independent t-test) comparing amplitudes.\n6. Plot violin or boxplots of amplitude by phoneme type and report summary statistics.","deliverables":"1. Test statistic, p-value, and effect size for vowel vs consonant amplitudes.\n2. Boxplots or violin plots comparing phoneme types.\n3. Table of mean ± SD by phoneme type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer word durations (as measured in the events.tsv duration field) are associated with delayed peak latency of the early evoked response.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether the acoustic duration of a word predicts the latency of its peak MEG response.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session’s events.tsv, select kind=='word' and read the duration field and timemeg.\n2. For each word, epoch MEG data from –0.1 to +0.5 s, baseline-correct using –0.1–0 s.\n3. For each epoch, compute the grand-average across channels and identify the latency (relative to timemeg) of the maximum absolute amplitude peak within 0.05–0.3 s.\n4. Compile (word_duration, peak_latency) pairs across sessions.\n5. Fit a regression of peak_latency ~ word_duration; report slope, CI, and p-value.\n6. Plot scatter of peak latency vs word duration with fitted line and histogram of word durations.","deliverables":"1. Regression summary for duration effect on latency.\n2. Scatter plot with trend line.\n3. Histogram of word durations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within-sentence temporal context (time since sentence start) modulates pre-onset theta-band (4–7 Hz) power at word onsets: later words show increased theta power reflecting integrative processing.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether theta-band power before word onsets increases as a function of timesentence.",  
      "steps":"1. Load all sessions’ events.tsv and select kind=='word'.\n2. For each word, read timesentence and timemeg.\n3. Extract MEG data from –0.2 to 0 s around timemeg, bandpass-filter in 4–7 Hz, compute analytic amplitude via Hilbert transform, and average over time and channels to get pre_theta_power.\n4. Compile (timesentence, pre_theta_power) pairs across sessions.\n5. Fit a linear regression or generalized additive model of pre_theta_power ~ timesentence; report statistics.\n6. Plot pre-onset theta power vs timesentence with a smoothing curve and histogram of timesentence values.","deliverables":"1. Model summary (slope or smooth term statistics, p-value).\n2. Scatter plot with smoothing line.\n3. Histogram of timesentence distribution."  
    }  
  }  
]}
